te when the Sun was  shining on a on a beautiful September  day but you know these are very scary  animals this is probably the last thing  that a yellowfin tuna sees before it's  lights out there are ambush predators  Makos you know attack from below they  have that big eye that allows them to  see very well in deep dark waters and to  be able to look up and and see those  patterns  and then when they see a prey fish they  come up and so often bite the tail off  of their prey whether it's a tuna or a  Marlin or something like that  and then wait for it to bleed out a  little bit circle around and then come  in and finish it off and those are the  things that are always foremost in my  mind when I'm swimming with a mako and  it's nipping at my fins  I've actually switched my fans I just  have these split fins that looked a  little bit like a fish and so now I've  gone with something that like too big  two by fours on my feet something that  looks very unfished like but you know  everything about this animal says  predator it's it's really built like a  torpedo with teeth just pure muscle and  all about all about feeding now I also  wanted a share sort of a little inside  baseball story about shark diving you  know a lot of shark operators over the  last few years have taken to using music  on their boat to try to attract certain  species of sharks as places where they  do that with great whites and Australia  other places for blue sharks and so  forth but Makos are very elusive I've  been trying to dive with Makos for  decades and had very little success I  mean on this story I had some luck but  they're very hard to get to come close  to the boat and a lot of times when they  do come close they're just in for a few  seconds or a minute and then and then  they're gone so I've had my best luck by  trying the music thing you know I  actually went out there and tried music  but what I've learned was that the only  music that actually works for Makos is a  CDC these are just badass sharks I mean  that's just that's just all there is to  it  and I'm not kidding I mean we've been  out there I've been out in San Diego and  New Zealand for days you know when  nothing was happening  and I look at my assistants say you know  time for a little ac/dc and not every  time but a lot of times you see a mako  come in so this was one in New Zealand  which is sort of Makos central or so  they call him there Matt Oh Matt go  central there's a lot of them around and  there are days where we had maybe a  dozen essaouira around us and water it  gets a little too crazy when that  happens but you know even the little  ones this is another one in San Diego  even the little ones come  with attitude you know they're like they  come in hot and they're all fierce and  bitey  Mako's we'll just bite things they don't  have hands and there's just all jacked  up it's like they're on cocaine or  something you know not that I would know  but people have told me but and you know  that one had these copepods on its  dorsal fin and this was another one a  bigger animal that was swimming down in  that beautiful blue water off California  here and they they often get those  parasites on their on the dorsal looks  like little streamers kind of hanging  off there but this diving like the the  Tigers I always do without cages you  know it would just be impractical to try  to get photographs because the animals  are so unpredictable they don't stick  around very long  and they would almost never come over to  a cage so you have to sort of be out  there in the open and most times I'm  diving alone because I don't want even  one other person in the water that might  spook the shark but sometimes I'll have  an assistant in and sometimes you'll  carry just a little stick there's a sort  of a deterrent if the animal gets a  little too aggressive which has happened  a few times but for the most part they  they don't do that though they will come  in and and check you out a little bit  but these are these are animals that are  endothermic meaning that they can  generate heat in their body it was  Brooke Burnett actually a National  Geographic who who turned the phrase hot  sharks these are hot sharks because they  can generate heat in their body and  because of that they can swim into  colder water and feed on oilier fish and  so forth but the mako is also one of the  fastest animals in the ocean a mako  shark can swim at bursts of over 45  miles an hour underwater in fast bursts  and you kind of remember that underwater  that the medium is much thicker than in  air so that's probably like swim in 100  miles an hour or something but  extraordinary animals and in in San  Diego I wanted to try to hang out near  kelp patties in the kelp forests of  California kelp giant kelp will often  break off and mat up and and drift along  the coast in its own little ecosystem  forms we get little fish and then bigger  fish and sometimes you get Dorado  mahi-mahi and fish like that and I spent  weeks out there trying to get a picture  of a mako near one and that's what we  see hunt and didn't actually get them  predating but but I don't think anything  like this had ever been made  so that was a kind of a cool thing as  well but you know these are the moments  I dream about right here for a when a  big ol mako shark comes in and bites the  crap out of my down port fetch that's  heaven but you know I honestly try to  resist the temptation of making scary  pictures of sharks because I really  don't want to perpetuate that myth and  enough damage has been done to sharks  over the years but by the same token I  don't want to sort of portray them as  something that they're not I don't want  to show them as house pets or give the  impression that you know they're cuddly  and we can go out and hug a shark  because they're not their real predators  so I think you know we have to walk that  that fine line and and show them for  what they are but but not over demonize  them for sure and with the same story I  also wanted to spend some time looking  at shark tournaments because you know  there's still a lot of this thing going  on where there will be these monster  shark tournaments held in various places  so this summer I spent a couple of days  in Montauk New York where they they hold  these tournaments for a couple of days  and they give prize money for the  biggest shark killed and it oftentimes  is a mako or a thresher but you know I  think that the time has passed for this  kind of thing when you consider that a  hundred million sharks are being killed  every year I don't really think there's  a benefit to sort of glamourizing  killing these animals in the two days  that I was in in Montauk I saw six  sharks come in no I'm sorry twelve there  was six Makos and six threshers and  every single one of them was a juvenile  every single one wasn't even old enough  to reproduce and you know I watched them  drag these things up on the on the dock  and sort of cut them up and I saw all  these children these kids that were  fascinated with sharks you know but I  think it's sort of unfortunate that this  is their first scene of a shark you know  looking like a slayed dragon just laid  there and that the fisherman was some  sort of heroes for doing this and I have  no problem with fishing but I just think  the time has passed for this sort of  treatment of sharks and you know I  recognize that not everyone will get to  see a shark underwater probably the way  that they should be seeing this amazing  Lima Jets majestic animal you know we we  can appreciate lions and grizzly bears  and tigers and so forth but underwater  these animals are just slaughtered and  often with little regard so my hope is  this photography can sort of move that  that forward a little bit in terms of  some appreciation and one of the other  things I wanted to do with with this  story with video actually was to show  the speed of a mako attacking something  so with the grant from the Geographic  Society I designed with my assistant at  Woods Hole Oceanographic we designed a  tow camera system I found an industrial  camera that shot at very high speed I  could shoot at 720 frames a second we  built this tow camera and I worked with  a New Zealand  champion sport fisherman who fished from  Marlin and he would rig a mullet bait  type of fish called mullet and trail it  behind the boat from Marlin but he said  oftentimes Makos would attack it so I  was there for a couple of weeks but I  only got one day where I could do it so  I've got a two-minute video with no  sound but I'll just have a look at that  and show you this is the first time this  has ever been seen of a mako and  high-speed attacking something a mullet  everything that you're going to see here  happened in about four seconds but we're  shooting so fast that we can play it  back at normal speed and it's it's slow  motion but it was fascinating you see  this animal after he latched on sort of  put his pectoral fins back in a very  hydrodynamic way almost trying to use  his weight and the speed to break it off  the boat was traveling at about eight  knots and he had no trouble of course  you know firing up and going in and  grabbing that bait but it was  interesting to see that behavior I think  researchers haven't showed this to  scientists yet but I think they'll be  interested to see this form that the  Mako assumes once he's bitten into the  prey just a truly remarkable predator  for sure  going for ride they can sort of detach  their jaws like a great white as well  and realize that if you can see it  coming out it's a pretty pre mazing  behavior from a mako shark shout at 720  frames a second
 

